      modelAnswer: 'Application software performs tasks for the end user — its purpose is the task itself. Examples include word processors (Microsoft Word), web browsers (Chrome), spreadsheets (Excel), media players, games and business applications. The user actively interacts with application software to get work done. Utility software, by contrast, is a category of system software that helps maintain, protect or optimise the computer itself rather than perform a user task. Examples include disk defragmenters, file-compression utilities (WinZip, 7-Zip), antivirus software, backup tools, system-cleaning utilities, file managers, and disk-imaging tools. Utility software typically runs in the background or is invoked occasionally to look after the computer; it is rarely the reason the user bought the machine, but the computer works less well without it. Both are distinct from the operating system itself, which provides the underlying platform on which both applications and utilities run.'
